---
import { getCollection } from 'astro:content';
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';

const byOrder = (a: { data: { order: number } }, b: { data: { order: number } }) => a.data.order - b.data.order;
const research = (await getCollection('projects', (p) => p.data.featured && !p.data.draft)).sort(byOrder);
const allWriting = (await getCollection('writing', (w) => !w.data.draft)).sort(byOrder);
const writing = allWriting.filter((w) => !w.data.personal).slice(0, 3);
const personal = allWriting.filter((w) => w.data.personal);
const meta = (w: (typeof allWriting)[number]) => [w.data.venue, w.data.role].filter(Boolean).join(' · ');

// a project links to its blog post if it has one, otherwise to its entry on /research
const href = (p: (typeof research)[number]) =>
  p.data.links.find((l) => !l.url.startsWith('TODO'))?.url ?? `/research/#${p.id}`;
---

<Base title="Ioana Frîncu (Frincu): research on AI evaluation and safety" jsonLd>
  <header class="hero">
    <div class="portrait-wrap" id="portrait">
      <div class="flip">
        <img class="portrait face front" src="/photo.jpg" width="168" height="168" alt="Portrait of Ioana Frîncu in a black bucket hat" />
        <img class="portrait face back" src="/meme.jpg" width="168" height="168" alt="The evil plotting raccoon meme: a raccoon rubbing its paws together" aria-hidden="true" loading="lazy" />
      </div>
      <button class="raccoon-btn" id="meme-toggle" type="button" aria-pressed="false" aria-label="Reveal my fav meme template" data-tip="reveal my fav meme template">
        <img src="/raccoon.svg" width="24" height="20" alt="" />
      </button>
    </div>
    <div>
      <h1 class="name">Ioana Frîncu</h1>
      <p class="links" id="contact">
        <a href="https://github.com/flifenstein">GitHub</a>
        <a href="https://www.linkedin.com/in/frincui">LinkedIn</a>
        <a href="https://scholar.google.com/citations?user=Y2m2NaoAAAAJ&hl=en">Scholar</a>
        <a href="https://flifenstein.com">Blog</a>
        <a href="/cv/">CV</a>
        <span>f_ioana [at] tuta [dot] com</span>
      </p>
      <p>
        I got curious about models that act differently when they think they are being evaluated, why they do it, how we capture this insigight and how to make the AI not decieve. It turned into an itch I can't stop scratching, so currently I work on it in a SPAR project and in my own studies (aka free time). Current plan is to apply to PhD programs in fall 2027, for 2028 entry where I can work on LLM evaluations and make the science around it more solid.
      </p>
      <p>
        By day I'm a product manager at aistack, imec's AI systems team Belgium, where we run differnent AI workloads on real
        hardware, picking up various new research challenges as we progress into building a useful product for optimizing the AI stack. Thank to my role and my own strengths, I'm also
        exploring technical AI governance on the compute side next to empirical safety research. Long term I'd like to continue enabling fantatsic teams to thrive and have imapct, but first my inner child wants a proper go at being a researcher and sticking with one problem. 
      </p>
      <p>
        Before imec I studied HCI at Twente and Aalto (EIT double degree Master) and worked at Microsoft's AI for Good Lab. Off the clock
        I hike long trails and play a gnome warlock. 
      </p>
    </div>
  </header>

  <section class="questions" aria-labelledby="questions">
    <h2 id="questions">Questions I want to work on</h2>
    <ul class="plain">
      <li>
        How can we detect when a language model is scheming, cheating or reward hacking, and use those signals to
        steer it away from doing so?
        <span class="take">I believe there is a way to see exactly how they do it. The traces are there, in the
        reasoning, the internals and the shortcuts they take, and better evals should turn them into something we can
        align against, not just report.</span>
      </li>
      <li>
        What would a technically feasible compute governance regime look like if it accounted for real AI workloads
        and inference, not only chips and data centres?
        <span class="take">imec taught me how chips, supply chains and verification actually work. I want to show
        where the EU's Chips Act 2.0 and Cloud and AI Development Act (CADA) proposals fail to connect to the AI Act,
        and what their AI parts would need to verify.</span>
      </li>
      <li>
        Which sandboxes and deployment practices make everyday use of AI safe by default, and how do we measure what
        AI is doing to people, societies and economies?
        <span class="take">Because right now, honestly, it's a mess.</span>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p class="stance">
      Where I stand: big fan of open-weight models, AI for good (I worked in the lab, so duh) and AI for science.
      Anti-AI for exactly one thing, my personal blog posts. I write those myself.
    </p>
  </section>

  <div class="cards">
    <section class="card" aria-labelledby="research">
      <h2 id="research">Research</h2>
      <ul class="plain">
        {
          research.map((p) => (
            <li>
              <a href={href(p)}>{p.data.title}</a>
              <span class="meta">{p.data.venue ?? p.data.context}, {p.data.year}</span>
            </li>
          ))
        }
      </ul>
      <p class="mono"><a href="/research/">All research →</a></p>
    </section>

    <section class="card" aria-labelledby="writing">
      <h2 id="writing">Writing</h2>
      <p class="label">At aistack, while working at imec</p>
      <ul class="plain">
        {
          writing.map((w) => (
            <li>
              <a href={w.data.url}>{w.data.title}</a>
              <span class="meta">{meta(w)}</span>
            </li>
          ))
        }
      </ul>
      <p class="label sep">Personal blog</p>
      <ul class="plain">
        {
          personal.map((w) => (
            <li>
              <a href={w.data.url}>{w.data.title}</a>
            </li>
          ))
        }
      </ul>
      <p class="mono"><a href="/writing/">All writing →</a></p>
    </section>
  </div>
  <script is:inline>
    (function () {
      var wrap = document.getElementById('portrait');
      var btn = document.getElementById('meme-toggle');
      var front = wrap.querySelector('.front');
      var back = wrap.querySelector('.back');
      btn.addEventListener('click', function () {
        var on = wrap.classList.toggle('flipped');
        btn.setAttribute('aria-pressed', String(on));
        btn.dataset.tip = on ? 'back to me' : 'reveal my fav meme template';
        front.setAttribute('aria-hidden', String(on));
        back.setAttribute('aria-hidden', String(!on));
      });
    })();
  </script>
</Base>
